WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
Alright.

00:04.320 --> 00:09.880
The fifth best practices and the most important one is avoid overindexing, because too many indexes

00:09.880 --> 00:15.080
are going to slow down the insert update delete operations, and it's going to confuse as well the execution

00:15.080 --> 00:19.800
plan about choosing the right index and the performance of the whole system is going to go down.

00:19.800 --> 00:23.240
And another tip is to monitor the usage of the indexes.

00:23.240 --> 00:29.360
And I can tell you 90% of the indexes that is being created usually are not used at all.

00:29.360 --> 00:32.680
So they are taking a lot of space, slowing down everything.

00:32.840 --> 00:36.360
So go and drop those unused indexes in your system.

00:36.360 --> 00:41.040
The next best practice is to have a regular job, like maybe a weekly job.

00:41.040 --> 00:46.840
So first you have to update the statistics regularly as you are inserting new data and modifying data

00:46.840 --> 00:51.880
inside your database, the statistics and the metadata of your tables might get outdated.

00:51.880 --> 00:56.560
And this is really bad because you will not get an optimal execution plan for your queries, and this

00:56.560 --> 00:58.040
is going to slow down your queries.

00:58.040 --> 00:58.560
Of course.

00:58.560 --> 01:04.960
So regularly make sure that all the statistics are updated in order to have an optimal execution plan.

01:04.960 --> 01:11.480
And what else we can do in this weekly job is that we can go and rebuild and reorganize our indexes,

01:11.520 --> 01:16.440
and that is to make sure that we are preventing data fragmentations in our indexes.

01:16.440 --> 01:21.520
Data fragmentations in your indexes is really bad because there will be a lot of unused spaces.

01:21.520 --> 01:24.440
The order of your clustered index will not be correct.

01:24.440 --> 01:29.640
So make sure that at least weekly you are rebuilding and reorganizing all your indexes.

01:29.640 --> 01:34.640
So those are the best practices of improving the performance and optimizing your indexing.

01:34.640 --> 01:40.560
If you are struggling with very large tables in your projects, like having fact tables, then go and

01:40.560 --> 01:48.720
use SQL partitioning in order to divide these tables into smaller pieces, which can improve the performance.

01:48.720 --> 01:52.120
Whether you are reading data from the table or writing data.

01:52.160 --> 01:59.320
And of course, you can go and mix things where you can go and apply a columnstore index on this partition

01:59.320 --> 01:59.920
table.

02:00.160 --> 02:04.360
Then you will get the best performance if you are having large tables.
